            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the clarification!\n\n> the description mentions that but here's a sequence with the default config:\n\nHere is what description mentions: \"In a Markdown document type \\`hello , at this point if you type another \\` it will insert (2) \\`\\` instead of just (1) \\` leaving you with \\`hello\\`\\` \". This should not happen, as explained in my previous comment.\n\n> 1. Insert this text into a Markdown file:\n> ...      \n> 2. Put your cursor where the `|` is:\n> ...      \n> 3. Enter in `, at this point `mini.pairs` will change the text to:\n> ... \n> 4. Put your cursor where the `|` is:\n> ... \n> 5. Enter in `, at this point `mini.pairs` will change the text to this and put the cursor in the middle:\n\nThis all works as expected and [documented](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/d570e0b01aabfe949c097983ed6386af60db32ed/doc/mini-pairs.txt#L9-L10): 'min.pairs' \"knows\" only about immediate cursor neighborhood when it decides whether to plain symbol or do an extra action (open, close, closeopen, `<BS>`, `<CR>`). In this scenario the left `` ` `` is too far away so 'mini.pairs' inserts the whole pair.\n\nThis whole 'mini.pairs' design is oriented more towards working with paired characters during actually typing things. For the described scenario of editing an existing text, 'mini.ai' (to select and act between paired characters) and 'mini.surround' (add/delete/replace paired strings) are more appropriate.\n\nFor example, surrounding \"world\" with backticks/quotes is done by placing cursor on any character in \"world\" and typing `sa` (\"surround add\" which will start an operator, like built-in `d` / `y` / etc.) + `iw` (\"inside word\" which mean \"world\" in this scenario) + `` ` `` (surrounding with identifier `` ` `` which will add `` ` `` on the left and right side of \"world\").\n\nClosing as working as expected.",
