        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> Although it works perfectly with less overhead in the use cases it is designed for.\n\nMaybe when I get more used to Vim and always use `mini.ai` I will switch back. For now there's sometimes more mental overhead for me to use it instead of typing the characters manually.\n\n> Typing saiw' to surround a word with quotes is arguably easier than i<C-v>'<Esc>ea<C-v>'<Esc> (<C-v> are needed to actually insert a single character), that's why it is the recommended approach here.\n\nI never used the `<C-v>` approach. I would just type:\n\n```\nHello world\n```\n\nThen go back to world and put in the \\` before the \"w\" and then do the same after the \"d\".\n\nUsing `saiw'` would be easier perhaps, but what happens when you have a few words such as:\n\n```\nHello world this is a sentence\n```\n\nAnd now you want \"this is a sentence\" to be wrapped in quotes. You have to go back to using a visual selection right?",
