        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Makes sense. I think that might be why I am not using mini.ai or mini.surround enough. I've only ever used it when an active visual selection is happening which required a lot of keystrokes. I'm going to try and train my brain to use it more in normal mode since the idea of using it would be really efficient in a lot of cases.\n\nI do happen to use https://github.com/folke/flash.nvim which comes with LazyVim for smart jumping. I see it works to set up the visual selection and then surround's keymaps work. Would operator targeting not work here since when you press \"g\", it's expecting \"s\" to start surround mode not enable Flash? For reference Flash uses \"s\" to be invoked and LazyVim set \"gs\" for mini.surround to avoid the keymap conflict.\n\nHonestly, just playing around and entering visual selection mode, then tapping `w` a few times to span a few words, hitting backspace twice to get rid of the trailing space in the selection and then doing `gsa\"` is not bad too. I feel like this is already faster than my old approach of manually typing things which resulted in step 5's issue with this plugin.\n\nThank you for taking the time out to answer these questions.",
